://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rewa_Kantha_Agency_with_all_Princely_States_Gujarat_during_British_India_1811-1937_with_labels.svg"}},"judicate-of-logudoro":{"t":"The northern Sardinian kingdom of Torres, one of four native judicates that ruled the island after Byzantine authority faded and a patron of Sardinia\u0027s finest Romanesque churches.","o":"As Byzantine control over Sardinia lapsed in the 10th and early 11th centuries, the island divided into four self-governing realms ruled by judges (judikes). Logudoro, also called Torres after the old Roman port of Porto Torres, covered the north-west. Its rulers held court at Ardara and appear in documents from the early 11th century.","r":"The judge ruled with an assembly of notables, the corona de logu, and governed through curatorie, rural districts. Land registers called condaghes were kept in Sardinian, among the earliest texts in the language. Judges invited Benedictine, Camaldolese and Vallombrosan monks, who built churches such as the Santissima Trinità di Saccargia, while Pisa and Genoa competed for its trade and favour.","e":"The last ruler, Adelasia, married Enzo, son of Emperor Frederick II, but died without heirs in 1259. The realm was then carved up between the Doria and Malaspina families, the Judicate of Arborea and the town of Sassari, which became the Republic of Sassari.","ru":["Gonario II of Torres","Barisone II of Torres","Adelasia of Torres"],"re":"Catholic Christianity","la":"Sardinian","c":"medium","img":"/images/states/judicate-of-logudoro.jpg","cr":{"a":"Facquis","l":"CC BY-SA 4.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Judicate_of_Torres_(XI-XIV_centuries).svg"}},"jin-korean-state":{"img":"/images/states/jin-korean-state.jpg","cr":{"a":"Asieon","l":"CC0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jin_State_Korea.png"}},"judicate-of-cagliari":{"img":"/images/states/judicate-of-cagliari.jpg","cr":{"a":"Facquis","l":"CC BY-SA 4.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Judicate